        <h3>Key Strategic Takeaways</h3>
        <ul class='grid-layout two-cols gap gap-lg'>
          <li>
            <span class='takeaway-index'>01</span>
            <h4>Continuity Is Product Infrastructure</h4>
            <p>
              Documentation, explicit contracts and careful onboarding are not
              administrative extras. They protect the reasoning behind the
              product when teams and technologies change.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class='takeaway-index'>02</span>
            <h4>Reliability Is Part of the UX</h4>
            <p>
              Reconnection, cache invalidation, teardown and backward
              compatibility are experienced by users as trust. Product quality
              lives between layers, not only on the screen.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class='takeaway-index'>03</span>
            <h4>Proximity Speeds Up Learning</h4>
            <p>
              Owning product direction, UX and frontend implementation shortened
              the distance between a field insight, a design decision and
              production feedback, without treating engineering as mere
              delivery.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class='takeaway-index'>04</span>
            <h4>Depth Creates Better Simplicity</h4>
            <p>
              The simplest participant interaction can sit on top of complex
              energy, billing and synchronization rules. Understanding those
              rules deeply is what makes it possible to hide the right
              complexity.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div id='road-ahead' class='content-wrapper flex-layout column gap'>
        <h3>The Road Ahead</h3>
        <p>
